public void processArgs(Option opt, ListIterator iter) throws ParseException
{
    // loop until an option is found
    while (iter.hasNext())
    {
        String str = (String) iter.next();

        // found a value
        try
        {
            opt.addValueForProcessing(Util.stripLeadingAndTrailingQuotes(str));
        }
        catch (RuntimeException exp)
        {
            iter.previous();
            break;
        }
    }

    if (opt.getValues() == null && !opt.hasOptionalArg())
    {
        throw new MissingArgumentException(opt);
    }
}
```

The `stripLeadingAndTrailingQuotes` method is used to process argument values before adding them for processing. It appears that leading and trailing quotes are being stripped from the argument values before further processing in `Parser.processArgs`.

Based on the current bug report, it seems that the behavior expected by the user is that trailing double quotes in the argument values should be retained. However, the current implementation removes leading and trailing quotes.

To address the issue reported, the `stripLeadingAndTrailingQuotes` method may need to be modified to preserve trailing double quotes if they are part of the argument values.

